1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,452
Welcome to practicals one and two.

2
00:00:02,452 --> 00:00:06,203
Today we go from a vague question to a distribution.

3
00:00:06,203 --> 00:00:13,488
We frame the problem, load data, name our variables, describe the numbers, and visualise their shape.

4
00:00:14,288 --> 00:00:16,195
The path has four stages.

5
00:00:16,195 --> 00:00:17,338
Frame and load:

6
00:00:17,338 --> 00:00:22,067
write a problem statement, then take a first look at the data.

7
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:23,592
Name your variables:

8
00:00:23,592 --> 00:00:27,405
what you are explaining and what might explain it.

9
00:00:27,405 --> 00:00:29,007
Describe the numbers:

10
00:00:29,007 --> 00:00:32,972
mean versus median, variance versus I Q R, skewness.

11
00:00:32,972 --> 00:00:38,768
And see the shape with histograms, boxplots, density plots and pivot tables.

12
00:00:39,568 --> 00:00:43,808
Before you load a single row, put your question into words.

13
00:00:43,808 --> 00:00:45,892
What question am I answering?

14
00:00:45,892 --> 00:00:47,832
What is one row in my data?

15
00:00:47,832 --> 00:00:49,916
What outcome am I explaining?

16
00:00:49,916 --> 00:00:52,719
And what would an answer let me decide?

17
00:00:52,719 --> 00:00:53,581
For example:

18
00:00:53,581 --> 00:00:59,690
do customers who receive a discount email spend more per month than those who do not?

19
00:00:59,690 --> 00:01:04,936
A problem statement earns its place only if the answer changes an action.

20
00:01:05,736 --> 00:01:09,353
Now load the data and confirm what actually arrived.

21
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:10,118
Five steps:

22
00:01:10,118 --> 00:01:19,369
read the file with read C S V, check the shape, preview rows with head, check data types, and count missing values with isna dot sum.

23
00:01:19,369 --> 00:01:28,968
Do this every time, even for a dataset you have seen before, because column names, types and row counts change more often than you expect.

24
00:01:29,768 --> 00:01:34,794
Next, decide what you are trying to explain and what might explain it.

25
00:01:34,794 --> 00:01:40,969
The dependent variable, Y, is the outcome, such as monthly spend, exam score or churn.

26
00:01:40,969 --> 00:01:48,940
The independent variables, X, are the factors that might influence it, such as a discount email or study hours.

27
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:50,017
The quick test:

28
00:01:50,017 --> 00:01:56,192
if I built a chart with this on the y axis, would the rest of my analysis point at it?

29
00:01:56,992 --> 00:02:02,692
Our running example is six hundred customers' monthly spend, which is right-skewed.

30
00:02:02,692 --> 00:02:08,392
The mean is thirty eight sixty three dollars; the median is thirty four fifty nine.

31
00:02:08,392 --> 00:02:13,268
A few big spenders drag the mean upward, while the median barely moves.

32
00:02:13,268 --> 00:02:17,320
Mean greater than median tells us the data is pulled right.

33
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:25,920
For spread we compare standard deviation, twenty dollars fifty five, with the I Q R, twenty three fifty.

34
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:30,795
Standard deviation uses every value and is sensitive to outliers.

35
00:02:30,795 --> 00:02:34,995
The I Q R covers the middle fifty percent and is robust.

36
00:02:34,995 --> 00:02:36,045
Rule of thumb:

37
00:02:36,045 --> 00:02:40,920
if the data has outliers or skew, lead with the median and I Q R.

38
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:47,282
You can read the shape without plotting, just by comparing mean and median.

39
00:02:47,282 --> 00:02:50,323
Mean below median means a long left tail.

40
00:02:50,323 --> 00:02:53,437
Mean about equal to median means balanced.

41
00:02:53,437 --> 00:02:56,552
Mean above median means a long right tail.

42
00:02:57,352 --> 00:03:00,149
Plot the same distribution three ways.

43
00:03:00,149 --> 00:03:03,608
A histogram is best for spotting overall shape.

44
00:03:03,608 --> 00:03:07,730
A boxplot shows the median and I Q R and flags outliers.

45
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:13,691
A density curve is a smoothed histogram, best for comparing shapes across groups.

46
00:03:13,691 --> 00:03:18,328
Each view highlights something different, so use more than one.

47
00:03:19,128 --> 00:03:20,762
A pivot table answers:

48
00:03:20,762 --> 00:03:25,293
what is the average, total or count, broken down by category?

49
00:03:25,293 --> 00:03:29,156
You choose rows, columns, values and an aggregation.

50
00:03:29,156 --> 00:03:38,069
In this example, East sells more widgets than gadgets while South is the reverse, a pattern a single average would hide.

51
00:03:38,069 --> 00:03:41,040
In pandas it is one call to pivot table.

52
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,939
Here is your cheat sheet.

53
00:03:43,939 --> 00:03:46,710
Question and unit of observation:

54
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:48,222
problem statement.

55
00:03:48,222 --> 00:03:50,321
Explain versus explainer:

56
00:03:50,321 --> 00:03:53,344
dependent and independent variables.

57
00:03:53,344 --> 00:03:56,703
Is the typical value pulled by outliers:

58
00:03:56,703 --> 00:03:58,718
compare mean and median.

59
00:03:58,718 --> 00:04:00,482
Is the spread robust:

60
00:04:00,482 --> 00:04:03,589
compare standard deviation and I Q R.

61
00:04:03,589 --> 00:04:05,520
Which way does it lean:

62
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:06,276
skewness.

63
00:04:06,276 --> 00:04:08,207
What does it look like:

64
00:04:08,207 --> 00:04:10,558
histogram, boxplot, density.

65
00:04:10,558 --> 00:04:12,658
And the summary by group:

66
00:04:12,658 --> 00:04:13,833
a pivot table.

67
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:16,353
Try these on your own dataset.

68
00:04:16,353 --> 00:04:18,032
Thanks for watching.

